One need to weigh two models:
1. Material + labour model for budget friendly but quality output; with control on cost and materials to use; flexible with additions during the execution; they charge labour on per Sft and materials on actuals;
2. Turnkey model: give every thing to interior company- for any changes during the execution, they charge above the estimate; material usage and cost not in your control; they load commissions on materials and labour; costs 30-40% more than the 1 st model;

For interiors: use an independent designer, pay them separately; this gives you clarity to negotiate with interior guys; also if you control material procurement on your own, this gives significant advantage on cost and freedom to choose material of your quality; 
it is very easy task, just visit local plywood shop and discuss with him, he gives an independent inputs, so you can use that info to discuss with your interior guys;

These two factors as owners shall spare little time, cannot outsource thinking hassle free; if these are outsourced to interior company they control the narrative from design such that they save on material cost and maximise profit, but sound reqsonable;

As mental modal is if we outsource end to end it is hassle free, and just check periodically; but interiors guy prefers to minimal designs such that he incur less material cost; 
They want to give under budget and sound reasonable, so they undercut designs and so the work involved;

If you control design and material, you save 40% of the cost; or you could use  superior materials; remember interiors is only woodwork and pop, lights; there are other expenses, curtains, Acs, Geysers, Hob, chimney, etc; 
if owner put efforts, within the same budget one pay to interior company, can get all these..
